            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>What to Do First After Account Suspension</h2>
<p>Don't rush to appeal. Log into Google Ads, navigate to "Account Status," and download the complete review notification email. The email will specify the violation type: trademark infringement, non-compliant landing page, or payment issue. Without this information, all your subsequent material preparation is blind guessing.</p>
<p>A tool app team once had their account suspended the day after adjusting their landing page. The reason was that the privacy policy link was missing from the page footer. The review notification clearly stated this, but the operations staff, eager to reopen the account, ignored this detail and wasted their first appeal opportunity.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Three Types of Materials You Must Include</h2>
<p><strong>1. Business Qualification Documents</strong></p>
<p>Business license, legal representative ID, ICP filing, or local operating permits. The review team verifies whether your business entity matches the account information. A cross-border e-commerce team once used a personal account to run ads for a business product, got rejected, and only then realized they needed to switch to a business account, which took another week.</p>
<p><strong>2. Rectification Statement</strong></p>
<p>Not "I was wrong," but "I was wrong about X, I've already fixed it, and here's how I'll prevent it from happening again." The review team wants to see genuine understanding of the rules, not templated apologies.</p>
<p><strong>3. Supporting Evidence</strong></p>
<p>Screenshots of the corrected landing page, updated privacy policy, third-party certifications, product compliance reports. The more specific your materials, the higher your approval rate. Saying "rectified" with no evidence is far less convincing than a before-and-after comparison image.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Can I Keep Running Ads During Review</h2>
<p>No. Once your account enters review status, all ads automatically pause. If you create new campaigns or modify budgets during review, the review team will see this as evasion and may impose harsher penalties.</p>
<p><strong>Correct approach</strong>: Keep the account inactive, prepare your materials, and wait for results. Review cycles typically take 1-3 business days, complex cases may extend to 5 days.</p>
<hr />
<h2>What If No Response After Appeal Submission</h2>
<p>For first-time appeals, wait at least 3 business days. If there's no response, contact Google Ads official support to check progress, provide your appeal reference number, and request manual follow-up.</p>
<p>Do not submit appeals repeatedly in a short period. Duplicate submissions get flagged by the system as spam requests, actually slowing down the review process. One e-commerce team, anxious about delays, submitted 4 appeals over two days. Their account was moved to "deep review" status, extending their suspension from 3 days to two weeks.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Can Multiple Suspensions Be Fixed</h2>
<p>Second-time suspensions are significantly harder to appeal. The review team accesses your account history, including previous violations and resolution records. If this is your third suspension notice, conduct an internal review first: Is there a systematic compliance gap, rather than just surface-level issues?</p>
<p>For repeatedly suspended accounts, consider preparing a "Compliance Rectification Plan" document detailing your understanding of Google Ads policies, internal review processes, and preventive measures. While not an appeal material itself, proactively sharing this document when communicating with support demonstrates your genuine commitment to compliance.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Three Common Pitfalls in Material Preparation</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Incorrect file formats</strong>: Google Ads accepts PDF, JPG, PNG, with files under 10MB each. ZIP archives or Word documents may be auto-filtered.</li>
<li><strong>Materials don't match violation types</strong>: Submitting a business license for trademark violations, or an ID for landing page issues, is ineffective.</li>
<li><strong>Vague rectification statements</strong>: "I deeply recognize my mistake" is far weaker than "I've moved the privacy policy link from the page footer to a prominent position in the navigation bar."</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Key Takeaways</h2>
<p>Account suspension isn't the end, but the appeal window is limited. Prepare qualification documents, clear rectification statements, and specific supporting evidence. Address each point in the review notification. Avoid repeated submissions and account operations during review. Wait patiently for results.</p>
<p>If your account involves sensitive industries (finance, healthcare, gaming, etc.) or has violation history, consult professionals before appealing to reduce the risk of repeated rejections.</p>
